#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092028 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b03546f-6c79-53b9-b284-fb23ae4dba52" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "1957007f-eabd-5d40-8f59-ff635ea1d539" :question/math-academy-id "e-602" :question/problem "Find an integral expression for the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/997/Source/Images/c85ca457e7f02850924980f54ba585bf.png)\n\n$x=1-t, \\quad y= \\dfrac{2}{3} t^{3/2}, \\qquad t\\in[0,1]$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\:\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(1 - t) =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{2}{3}t^{3/2}) = t^{1/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\int _{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}\\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{1}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2} + (t^{1/2})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1 + t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092029 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c82bbc8-83bc-55f8-be32-4d63f6db7261" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "86835544-a7f1-5be2-99c1-14e39a14f3fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-603" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$x=e^t(\\cos{t}+\\sin{t}), \\quad y=e^t(\\cos{t}-\\sin{t}), \\quad t\\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right]$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/997/Source/Images/01cb59beb0b1df4a0cf0402429bafa19.png)" :question/worked-solution "Computing the derivatives of $x$ and $y$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{t}(\\cos t + \\sin t)) \\\\\n&= e^{t}(\\cos t + \\sin t) + e^{t}(-\\sin t + \\cos t) \\\\\n&= 2e^{t}\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{t}(\\cos t - \\sin t)) \\\\\n&= e^{t}(\\cos t - \\sin t) + e^{t}(-\\sin t - \\cos t) \\\\\n&=-2e^{t}\\sin t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\int _{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}\\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(2e^{t}\\cos t)^{2} + (-2e^{t}\\sin t)^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{4e^{2t}(\\cos^{2} t + \\sin^{2} t)}dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}2e^{t}dt \\\\\n&= 2e^{t} \\mid _{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\\n&= 2(e^{\\pi /4} - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092060 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebd2a5c8-5d15-589d-81c9-9b67f4e8f0b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "5bae6269-2112-56ea-b123-bee58701cf87" :question/math-academy-id "e-7130" :question/problem "Find the expression that gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=t, \\, y=\\ln{(\\cos{t})}$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (see graph below).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/997/Source/Images/f10352be16212d467425c8695c797467.png)" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\:\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t) = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\ln (\\cos t)) =-\\tan t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\int _{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}\\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(1)^{2} + (-\\tan t)^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1 + \\tan^{2} t}dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{\\sec^{2} t}dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sec tdt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c82bbc8-83bc-55f8-be32-4d63f6db7261" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dacc9401-1031-587a-95d4-c464243626b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186088945} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e58f09-1485-5c23-98aa-969b9128ba76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186088946} {:answer/id #uuid "ea5768ae-4c10-5d95-942b-92975bb3164d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186088947} {:answer/id #uuid "ec697b34-c934-52b0-baab-688d2f5275af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186088948} {:answer/id #uuid "1ac661cb-8ef9-5df6-bb9e-76539722f4dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186088949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ac661cb-8ef9-5df6-bb9e-76539722f4dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186088949} :answer-field/id #uuid "e140ed00-86f5-526b-b26f-8ad2e36a07be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186088944}] :question/id #uuid "dcf46b6f-f5d9-5eec-afdf-9ca4614f945b" :question/math-academy-id "q-10255" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/997/Source/Images/q-10255.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = t - \\sin t, y = 1 - \\cos t, t \\in  [0, \\pi ]$."}]}
